REGIONAL REGULATION OF KUDUS REGENCY

NUMBER 2 YEAR

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR VICTIMS OF GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

THE REGENT OF KUDUS,

Considering:

a. that all forms of violence, especially gender-based violence and violence against children are violations of human rights and crimes against human dignity as well as forms of discrimination;
b. that victims of gender-based violence and children must receive protection, both from the Regional Government, related agencies, and/or the community in order to be protected and free from violence and/or threats of violence in the household and community;
c. that based on Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection and Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence, the Regional Government, related agencies, and/or the community are obligated to undertake prevention, protection, and recovery efforts for victims of gender-based violence and children;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a, b, and c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 13 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Areas within the Province of Central Java;
3. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);
6. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4281);
7. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
8. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 125, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437 as amended several times most recently by Law Number 12 of 2008 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
9. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
10. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
11. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4967);
12. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);
13. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
14. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
15. Government Regulation Number 2 of 1988 concerning Child Welfare Efforts for Children with Problems (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1988 Number 2, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 3367);
16. Government Regulation Number 6 of 1988 concerning Coordination of Vertical Agency Activities in the Regions (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1988 Number 10, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 3373);
17. Government Regulation Number 79 of 2005 concerning Guidelines for the Development and Supervision of Regional Government Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2005 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4593);
18. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
19. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning the Division of Government Affairs Between the Government, Provincial Regional Government, and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
20. Government Regulation Number 50 of 2007 concerning Procedures for the Implementation of Regional Cooperation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2007 Number 112, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4761);
21. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
22. Presidential Regulation Number 1 of 2007 concerning the Ratification, Promulgation and Dissemination of Legislation;
23. Regional Regulation of Central Java Province Number 3 of 2009 concerning the Implementation of Protection for Victims of Gender-Based Violence and Children (Regional Gazette of the Province Year 2009 Number 3, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Central Java Province Number 20);
24. Regional Regulation of Kudus Regency Number 3 of 2008 concerning Government Affairs that are the Authority of the Regional Government of Kudus Regency (Regional Gazette of Kudus Regency Year 2008 Number 3, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Kudus Regency Number 106);

With Joint Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF KUDUS REGENCY

and

THE REGENT OF KUDUS

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR VICTIMS OF GENDER-BASED VIOLENCE AND CHILDREN.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of victims of gender-based violence and children is based on Pancasila and grounded in the 1945 Constitution, as well as basic principles that include:
a. non-discrimination;
b. best interests of victims;
c. justice and gender equality;
d. victim protection;
e. survival, and growth and development of children;
f. respect for the opinions of children;
g. transparency;
h. integration;
i. empowering; and
j. confidentiality of victims.

Article 3

The objectives of this Regional Regulation are:
a. to prevent all forms of gender-based violence in the home, and violence against children that occurs in the household and/or community;
b. to provide services and protection;
c. to provide legal assistance;
d. to seek recovery and social reintegration; and
e. to provide a sense of security to victims.

CHAPTER III

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 4

1. Every victim of gender-based violence and children has the right to:
a. obtain protection for the security of themselves, their families, and their property, and be free from threats related to information that will be, is being, or has been given;
b. participate in the process of selecting and determining the form of protection and security support;
c. obtain information regarding case developments and court decisions;
d. obtain integrated services that are fast, appropriate, comfortable, and in accordance with needs;
e. recovery and social reintegration; and
f. obtain legal, psychological, spiritual, economic, social assistance and interpreters.
2. The rights of victims as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER IV

OBLIGATIONS AND AUTHORITIES OF THE REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

Article 5

1. In providing protection to victims of gender-based violence and children, the Regional Government has the obligation to:
a. prevent the occurrence of violence;
b. provide protection for victims of violence;
c. provide recovery services and social reintegration for victims;
d. encourage and increase community participation;
e. cooperate with service providers in efforts to prevent, protect and recover victims of violence;
f. supervise the implementation of services for victims involving the community; and
g. provide safe houses (shelters).
2. To carry out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government has the authority to:
a. formulate policies and programs on the elimination of gender-based violence and violence against children;
b. facilitate the implementation of integrated services;
c. provide facilities and infrastructure;
d. increase the capacity of service provider institutions;
e. coordinate and cooperate in the implementation of protection for victims of gender-based violence and violence against children;
f. encourage community participation; and
g. conduct monitoring and evaluation.
3. The Regional Government in carrying out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of parents, guardians, husbands, wives, or other persons who are legally responsible for the victim.

Article 7

1. The Integrated Services as referred to in Article 6 shall be established by the Regent in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
2. The Integrated Services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall consist of members from the following elements:  a. regional government;
b. non-governmental institutions;
c. health service institutions;
d. law enforcement apparatus;
e. professional personnel;
f. volunteer companions;
g. social workers; and/orh. religious leaders.
3. The Integrated Services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall have the duties to:
a. undertake prevention;
b. recovery and social reintegration;
c. provide legal protection;
d. coordinate and cooperate;
e. undertake efforts to increase community participation; and
f. monitoring and reporting.

Article 10

Recovery and Social Reintegration Efforts as referred to in Article 7 paragraph (3) letter b include:
a. providing physical recovery at health service institutions;
b. medicolegal services;
c. assisting the return of victims;
d. providing protection in Safe Houses (shelters), social rehabilitation centers, or other places desired by the victim;
e. providing psychosocial recovery and assistance;
f. providing spiritual guidance services; and
g. conducting family and community preparation, economic empowerment, and return to school and/or other educational institutions.

Article 13

1. Increasing Community Participation as referred to in Article 7 paragraph (3) letter e is to provide the widest possible opportunity for the community to participate in assisting the prevention and handling of gender-based violence and violence against children.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by:
a. providing protection for victims;
b. providing emergency assistance;
c. providing advocacy to victims and or the community regarding the handling of cases of gender-based violence and violence against children;
d. assisting in the process of submitting applications for protection orders;
e. providing assistance in the form of money, goods, or services; and/or
f. conveying information to the competent authorities related to cases of gender-based violence and violence against children.
